          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Double negation translation
            is a method for converting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valid"} valid
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            into propositions valid in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contradiction"} contradiction
            in a classical theory could be translated to the proof of a contradiction in a constructive theory.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+G%C3%B6del"} Kurt Gödel
            used this technique to show that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+arithmetic"} Heyting arithmetic
            are equiconsistent.
          %p
            Double negation translation was discovered independently by a number of mathematicians including
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+G%C3%B6del"} Kurt Gödel
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"} Gerhard Gentzen
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrey+Kolmogorov"} Andrey Kolmogorov
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Gödel–Gentzen negative translation
          %h2#construction Construction
          %p
            The traditional descriptions are highly syntactic, but can be motivated by recalling some conceptual relationships between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
            (which are algebras for classical propositional logic) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebras"} Heyting algebras
            (which are algebras for intuitionistic propositional logic).
          %h3#propositional_case Propositional case
          %p
            Recall from the article
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo →
                  %mi Heyt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \colon Bool \to Heyt
          %p
            is given objectwise by taking a Heyting algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            to the poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+elements"} regular elements
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in L
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = \neg \neg x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
            is a Boolean algebra, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg \colon L \to L_{\neg\neg}
            is a Heyting algebra homomorphism which is universal among Heyting algebra maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \to U B
            into Boolean algebras.
          %p
            In particular, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Heyt(S)
            is the free Heyting algebra on a set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi free
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %mi Bool
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \stackrel{free}{\to} Heyt \stackrel{F}{\to} Bool
          %p
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Heyt(S)_{\neg\neg}
            is the free Boolean algebra on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            Furthermore, it was shown in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            that for Heyting algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi L
                      %mo →
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon L \to L
                preserves finite
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi L
                      %mo →
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon L \to L
                preserves the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
                operation
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⇒
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
          %p
            Consequently, the inclusion of regular elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg} \to L
            also preserves meets and implications,
            = succeed "." do
              %strong strictly
          .num_thm
            %h6#glivenkos_theorem Glivenko’s Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \Rightarrow q
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tautology"} tautology
              in classical propositional logic, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\neg \neg p) \Rightarrow (\neg \neg q)
              is a tautology in intuitionistic propositional logic, and conversely.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Here
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
              expressions in variables
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i \in S
              over the signature
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0, 1, \vee, \wedge, \Rightarrow)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \Rightarrow q
              is a classical tautology means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq (p \Rightarrow q)
              holds when interpreted in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bool
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(S)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bool
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi Heyt
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo ¬
                          %mo ¬
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(S) = Heyt(S)_{\neg\neg}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq \neg\neg(p \Rightarrow q) = (\neg\neg p) \Rightarrow (\neg\neg q)
            %p
              when interpreted in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Heyt
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Heyt(S)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\neg \neg p) \Rightarrow (\neg\neg q)
              is an intuitionistic tautology.
          %p
            Continuing this thought: the join
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Heyt
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(S) = Heyt_{\neg\neg}
            is computed as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi Bool
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi Heyt
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee_{Bool} b = \neg\neg(a \vee_{Heyt} b)
          %p
            since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon Heyt(S) \to Heyt(S)_{\neg\neg}
            preserves joins (it is a left adjoint). Putting all this together, because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon Heyt(S) \to Heyt(S)_{\neg\neg}
            preserves Heyting algebra structure, we arrive at the following syntactic translation.
          #defn.un_def
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The double-negation translation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \mapsto p^{N}
              on term expressions in the theory of Heyting algebras
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is defined by induction as follows.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^N = \neg\neg x
                  for variables
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 0
                          %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0^N = 0
                  (since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi L
                        %mo →
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon L \to L
                  preserves
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 1
                          %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1^N = 1
                  (since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi L
                        %mo →
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon L \to L
                  preserves
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo ∧
                        %mi q
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi p
                          %mi N
                        %mo ∧
                        %msup
                          %mi q
                          %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p \wedge q)^N = p^N \wedge q^N
                  (since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi L
                        %mo →
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon L \to L
                  preserves the meet operation)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi q
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi p
                          %mi N
                        %mo ⇒
                        %msup
                          %mi q
                          %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p \Rightarrow q)^N = p^N \Rightarrow q^N
                  (since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi L
                        %mo →
                        %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg \colon L \to L
                  preserves the implication operation)
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi q
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi N
                        %mo =
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi p
                          %mi N
                        %mo ∨
                        %msup
                          %mi q
                          %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p \vee q)^N = \neg\neg(p^N \vee q^N)
                  \.
          %p
            Thus, by Glivenko’s theorem,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is a classical tautology if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^N
            is an intuitionistic tautology. This result may be extended to theories as well: suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is an intuitionistic theory or Heyting algebra, given by a presentation as a coequalizer in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Heyt
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Heyt
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mi Heyt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi L
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Heyt(T) \stackrel{\to}{\to} Heyt(S) \to L.
          %p
            Then, since the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \mapsto L_{\neg\neg}
            is a left adjoint, it takes this coequalizer to a coequalizer
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mi Bool
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool(T) \stackrel{\to}{\to} Bool(S) \to L_{\neg\neg}
          %p
            so that an term expression
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            is a theorem in the classical theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\neg\neg}
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^N
            is a theorem in the intuitionistic theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          %h3#firstorder_case First-order case
          %p
            Double negation translation for a formula,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+language"} first-order language
            is defined inductively by the following clauses:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^N = \neg \neg \phi
                , for atomic
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi ψ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi N
                      %mo ∧
                      %msup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi \wedge \psi)^N = \phi^N \wedge \psi^N
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi ψ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ¬
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi N
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo ¬
                      %msup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi \vee \psi)^N = \neg(\neg \phi^N \wedge \neg \psi^N)
                (or equivalently,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi N
                        %mo ∨
                        %msup
                          %mi ψ
                          %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg (\phi^N \vee \psi^N)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ψ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi N
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi \to \psi)^N = \phi^N \to \psi^N
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi ϕ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\neg \phi)^N = \neg \phi^N
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mi ϕ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\forall x \phi)^N = \forall x \phi^N
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi x
                      %mi ϕ
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi N
                      %mo =
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mo ¬
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\exists x \phi)^N = \neg \forall x \neg \phi^N
                (or equivalently,
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi x
                        %msup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg \neg \exists x \phi^N
          %h3#higherorder_case Higher-order case
          %p
            The basic idea here is that any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            gives rise to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_{\neg\neg}
          %h2#references References
          %p Informal exposition of a tiny aspect of this double negation business is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrej+Bauer"}> Andrej Bauer
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://math.andrej.com/2008/08/13/intuitionistic-mathematics-for-physics/"} Intuitionistic mathematics for physics
